\textbf{Dose-response: gradient flow duration vs.\ CAR.} Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response} plots CAR against the number of OCC gradient flow steps for the cutoff series (15K--45K, plus Detach at 0K and Full OCC at 50K). The relationship is non-monotonic with substantial seed-dependent variability: Cutoff@15K (1 seed) achieves the highest CAR (0.804) despite the shortest gradient flow period, while Cutoff@25K (2 seeds) shows the lowest (0.724). Multi-seed conditions (Cutoff@35K and Full OCC) cluster near the Vanilla baseline. Reverse Cutoff (late 15K) falls below the early-cutoff counterpart at the same gradient flow duration, consistent with the continuity hypothesis. The non-monotonicity and high variance across low-seed conditions underscore that transition \emph{timing} and \emph{seed sensitivity}---not gradient flow dose alone---drive cutoff outcomes.

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]{figures/dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Dose-response: CAR vs.\ OCC gradient flow duration. Blue circles: early-cutoff progression (gradient flow for the first $N$ steps, Detach mode thereafter). Red square: Reverse Cutoff (gradient flow for the last 15K steps). Gray band: Vanilla $\pm$ 1 SD. Seed counts annotated; error bars show cross-seed SD where available. The non-monotonic pattern and high variance for low-seed conditions suggest that transition timing matters more than gradient flow dose.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure}

